---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Page from '../../components/Page.astro';
import MainPageContent from '../../components/MainPageContent.astro';
---

<Base title="Style Manual - Open English Translation">
    <Header />
    <main class="main">
        <Page>
            <MainPageContent>
                <div>
                    <p>
                        In line with our desire for both openness and
                        transparency, we are including our own style manual
                        here, so you can also hold us to account if we fail to
                        meet it.
                    </p>
                    <h1>DRAFT OET Style</h1>
                    <p>
                        We are initially using a style based on British English
                        (BrE) style for the OET. Later we hope (as far as is
                        possible) to automatically generate an American English
                        (AmE) version of our OET style.
                    </p>
                    <h2>Headings</h2>
                    <p>
                        Headings generally only have initial capitals on the
                        first word. They should be concise. They do not have
                        punctuation even if they are a full clause. They do not
                        have footnote, cross-reference, glossary or any other
                        kind of caller. They are normally bold and a sans-serif
                        font that coordinates nicely with the (more usually
                        serifed) font of the body text.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Title</h3>
                    <p>
                        The title of the entire work, e.g. <i>Holy Scriptures</i
                        >, is bold, centred and has heading capitalisation.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Book heading</h3>
                    <p>
                        Each Bible book starts on a new page (not necessarily
                        RHS). The heading format is bold, centred, sentence
                        capitalisation but without punctuation. The longest
                        version of the name should be written at the beginning
                        of that book, e.g. ‘Good news about Yeshua the Messiah
                        as told by Matteo’. If the full name is more than two
                        words or so, then there should also be a short name e.g.
                        ‘Matteo’, suitable for the contents list and for at the
                        top of each page of that book.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Section heading</h3>
                    <p>
                        Section headings are not inspired Scripture, which we
                        attempt to show by placing them in a box separate from
                        the text proper. They should only have the first word
                        having initial capital letter, otherwise normal sentence
                        capitalisation.
                    </p>
                    <h2>Body text</h2>
                    <p>
                        Be aware when editing or translating, of the register of
                        the text, for example how formal it is, what kind of
                        genre. match the English you use to that register as far
                        as possible.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The text immediately following a heading should not
                        assume that the reader has read the heading. So, for
                        example, you should not use a pronoun in the first
                        sentence of a section to refer to someone mentioned in
                        the section heading. You should refer to them fully.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        It is conventional but not necessary to begin a new
                        paragraph when there is a change of speaker.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Punctuation</h3>
                    <p>
                        Punctuation is set in the style of the phrase or title
                        it belongs to.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Avoid double punctuation. When you have an abbreviation
                        with punctuation straight after it, it is usually better
                        to drop the full stop at the end of the abbreviation.
                        You definitely should drop it if the sentence
                        punctuation is a full stop. Likewise do not use a comma
                        or a full stop after an ellipsis. Question marks at the
                        end of a quote should not be followed by a comma even if
                        the enveloping sentence has not ended e.g. when the
                        quote formula is at the end of the sentence. When there
                        could be two punctuation marks in the same place, use
                        the stronger punctuation. <i>!</i> and <i>?</i> are stronger
                        than <i>,</i> and . A colon is stronger than a semi-colon.
                        AmE is comfortable with double and even triple punctuation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When you have a sentence inside a sentence, do not put a
                        full stop on the embedded sentence. This can occur with
                        quotes and with sentences in brackets.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Full stop</h4>
                    <p>
                        Do not put a full stop at the end of a sentence if that
                        puts it immediately after a dot of an abbreviation or
                        similar.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A full stop should be followed by a capital letter. Try
                        hard to avoid having a digit or symbol at the beginning
                        of a sentence. You can use a semicolon instead of a full
                        stop in this situation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Ellipsis marks (…) would not normally be found in
                        Scripture. They can be used to mark a gap in quoted
                        material, or an incomplete sentence where the ending is
                        left to the readers’ imagination e.g. ‘but so that all
                        righteousness might be fulfilled …’.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        An ellipsis at the end of a sentence does not have a
                        fourth dot after it, but does if the ellipsis is
                        followed by a quotation mark. A capital letter after an
                        ellipsis implies that a whole sentence has been omitted.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Comma</h4>
                    <p>
                        Normally commas will be the only punctuation inside a
                        sentence. In general add a comma when it will remove
                        ambiguity, or where you would expect a reader to pause
                        for a breath.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Non-restrictive relative clauses are comma’ed off e.g.
                        ‘The teams, who had uniforms, were ready to compete’.
                        Restrictive relative clauses are not comma’ed e.g. ‘The
                        teams who had uniforms were more confident’. Likewise
                        for a phrase or word.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Two main clauses should not be connected to each other
                        with just a comma (called a comma splice). Use a
                        conjunction, semicolon or colon.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When a comma comes immediately after an abbreviation
                        ending in a dot, remove the abbreviation's dot.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Semicolon</h4>
                    <p>
                        Use a semicolon when you have already used commas and
                        want to show a stronger division e.g. fruit with pips,
                        stones or kernels; plants with red leaves; grain heads
                        and pulses.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        You can use a semicolon instead of a full stop when the
                        next sentence begins with a symbol or a letter that
                        cannot be capitalised. Use an em dash without spaces
                        both to restate or expand a statement, and to separate
                        off a parenthetical statement.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Colon</h4>
                    <p>
                        A colon points forward, e.g. from a premise to a
                        conclusion, to introduce a list. What comes after need
                        not be able to stand as a separate sentence. Use a colon
                        to separate two clauses when one explains the other.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In BrE the first word after a colon is not capitalised
                        unless the colon is introducing an extended quotation.
                        AmE may capitalise the word if the colon introduces a
                        grammatically complete sentence.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Question mark</h4>
                    <p>
                        In indirect speech, you do not necesssarily need to put
                        a question mark when a question is implied. Do not use a
                        question mark when the intent of the utterance is not to
                        ask a question e.g. were you born in a tent, unless it
                        is needed it to convey the intonation of a polite
                        request e.g. do you mind closing the door? Never use
                        multiple question marks in Scripture. An appropriate
                        emoji would be less radical!
                    </p>
                    <h4>Exclamation mark</h4>
                    <p>
                        You can use an exclamation mark following emphatic
                        statements, commands and interjections expressing
                        emotion. It should be used very sparingly, and never
                        more than one at a time.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Hyphen</h4>
                    <p>
                        Hyphens are used less often now for compound words. Some
                        compounds can be either two words, one word or
                        hyphenated. BrE tends to using two words and AmE to
                        using one word. We should be consistent throughout the
                        books in which form we use for each compound.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        See the appendix for the various words about which
                        decisions have been made for the OET.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Compound words, when used adjectivally, should be
                        hyphenated e.g. three quarters of the people voted.
                        There was a two-thirds majority.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        You can hyphenate a prefix or suffix to avoid confusion.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Hyphenate spelled out numbers between twenty-one and
                        ninety-nine.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When capitalising hyphenated words in a heading,
                        traditionally you did not capitalise the word after the
                        hyphen unless it is always capitalised. Now it is more
                        usual to hyphenate that word as well so we will do that.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        [**We don’t need the following if the software is able
                        to automatically do the hyphenating for us without us
                        putting in potential hyphen points.] Hyphenating words
                        at the end of a line can be very helpful for laying out
                        text, especially when you are having two columns. With
                        hyphenated words, the reader needs more skill to put the
                        word back together in their mind. But reading lines of
                        very different lengths takes reading skill too. Aim to
                        hyphenate at a syllable break and ideally at a morpheme
                        break, e.g. mis-spent not miss-pent. Each part of the
                        word should have at least three letters. Neither part of
                        the word should have a meaning different from the
                        meaning of the word as a whole e.g. molest-ation not
                        mole-station. Aim to hyphenate so that the
                        pronunciation/stress rules will still produce the
                        correct pronunciation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A word that usually has a hyphen might be broken at the
                        end of a line. Traditionally, if it breaks at the
                        hyphen, then both the parts of the word have a hyphen
                        (one at the end of the first line and another at the
                        beginning of the next line). These days it is as common
                        to only have the hyphen at the end of the first part,
                        unless it is important for the reader to know that the
                        word is always hyphenated.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Dash</h4>
                    <p>
                        Sometimes a dash can be used as a less formal option
                        where a colon would otherwise be. There should not be
                        any punctuation immediately before a dash. Do not
                        capitalise a word after a dash even if it begins a
                        sentence, unless it is a word that is always capitalised
                        like a name.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The en dash is the width of an average letter. It is
                        used without spaces to show a range, to separate names
                        of different people, e.g. Myers–Briggs, instead of a
                        forward slash to show alternatives, with a similar
                        effect to <i>to</i> or <i>and</i>. Do not use it to
                        separate off an aside or afterthought: use an em dash
                        instead.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The em dash is twice the length of the en dash. We are
                        using it to both to restate or expand a statement, and
                        to separate off a parenthetical statement. (AmE and
                        Oxford use an em dash without spaces as a parenthetical
                        dash. Non-Oxford BrE publications use an en dash with a
                        space either side.)
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        An em dash can be used with a space either side to
                        indicate a word deliberately left unstated.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Forward slash</h4>
                    <p>
                        In text the forward slash generally means <i>or</i>, not <i
                            >and</i
                        >. A forward slash or a vertical rule can be used in
                        laying out poetry if necessary to show the break in a
                        line of poetry that has not been laid out in poetical
                        style. Apart from this poetical use, it is better not to
                        use forward slash as it makes it awkward to read that
                        text aloud.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Brackets</h4>
                    <p>
                        The punctuation at the end of a sentence comes inside
                        the brackets if the entire sentence is inside the
                        brackets. An opening bracket should not have a comma
                        immediately before it.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Round brackets ( ) are also called parentheses. They are
                        used for digressions and explanations. Avoid nested
                        brackets if at all possible. Consider using commas or
                        dashes instead of one set of brackets.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Square brackets [ ] (simply called brackets in UsE),
                        braces {} (also called curly brackets), aand angle brackets
                        < > are not usually used in the OET text. [**Note here any
                        context where these would be used.]
                    </p>
                    <h4>Apostrophe</h4>
                    <p>
                        For plurals use <i>’s</i> after singular nouns and indefinite
                        pronouns. Use the apostrophe without the <i>s</i> after plurals
                        ending in s. Possessive pronouns don’t have an apostrophe.
                        Don’t use a possessive apostrophe with <i>its</i> (meaning
                        'belonging to it'). <i>It’s</i> with an apostrophe means <i
                            >It is</i
                        >.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Do not use an apostrophe when the s at the end of the
                        word is marking plural rather than possessive. The only
                        exceptions are for when it is needed for clarity e.g.
                        dot your i's. Do not use an apostrophe at the beginning
                        of words that began life as contractions but are now
                        accepted words e.g. flu.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The apostrophe in front of a year expressed as two
                        numbers should be a closing-quotation style e.g. summer
                        of ’89.
                    </p>
                    <h4>Quotation marks</h4>
                    <p>
                        When quotation marks are enclosing clauses or sentences,
                        up to a paragraph, use double quotation marks. When
                        quotation marks are needed within a clause, to enclose a
                        phrase or word, use single quotation marks.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For a quotation within a quotation, use single quotation
                        marks. Avoid if at all possible nesting quotation marks
                        further than two layers e.g. by using indirect speech
                        instead.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When direct speech in quotation marks has more than one
                        paragraph, the convention is to start each paragraph
                        with a quotation mark (called a continuer) but to only
                        put an end quotation mark at the completion of the
                        entire utterance.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Extended quotations, especially for more formal
                        utterances, do not have quotation marks. Instead the
                        text is indented one em at both left and right margins
                        to mark the extent of the quotation. For direct speech
                        use a colon at the end of the introducing paragraph
                        rather than a full stop or comma.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If you wish, words used in an ironic sense can be in
                        quotes. It is easy to overuse this convention: only use
                        this if the irony will otherwise be lost. Likewise a
                        newly coined word can be enclosed in single quotation
                        marks the first time it is used in any one Bible book.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When referring to a document title use italics (\it)
                        e.g. Annals of Judea. When referring to the title of a
                        song or poem, of a chapter or part of a document, use
                        quotation marks e.g. ‘Dove in the dawn’. Besides an
                        initial capital on the first word, use ordinary sentence
                        capitalisation in document titles.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Quotation marks should not come outside punctuation
                        unless the scope of the punctuation is entirely inside
                        the quotation. So, for example, an ending-quotation mark
                        should come before a full stop unless the whole sentence
                        is inside the quotation. In AmE punctuation is placed
                        inside the ending-quotation mark regardless.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </MainPageContent>
        </Page>
    </main>
    <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
    .main {
        min-height: 85vh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
    }
</style>
